{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_x9g24n69","toolName":"read","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"def summarize_ledger(csv_text):
    """Total the amount column per category from a ledger export.

    Returns {category: total} with each total rounded to 2 decimals,
    using round-half-up (accounting convention), not Python's default
    round-half-to-even.

    Columns are date,category,description,amount. Category keys are
    lowercase with surrounding whitespace stripped. The description
    field may contain commas and RFC4180-escaped internal quotes
    (a doubled "" represents one literal ").

    Amounts may be quoted, may use $, EUR (€), or GBP (£) symbols,
    thousands separators, and may have a third decimal place (a sub-cent
    remainder from currency conversion). A negative amount is written
    either in accounting parentheses, e.g. (45.00), or with a leading
    minus sign.

    Rows with a missing or empty amount are skipped, as are blank lines.
    A row that does not have exactly four fields once quoting is
    accounted for (e.g. a trailing report/footer line) is not a data
    row and must be skipped without raising an error.
    """
    totals = {}
    for line in csv_text.strip().splitlines()[1:]:
        parts = line.split(",")
        cat = parts[1]
        amount = float(parts[3].replace("$", ""))
        totals[cat] = totals.get(cat, 0) + amount
    return {k: round(v, 2) for k, v in totals.items()}
"}]},"isError":false}
